{
  "dom_id": "step-q213591",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nThe relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, for any real numbers $x$ and $y,$ if ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ then ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nthe relation is $\\text{symmetric}.$\n\n**Note:** $R$ is not antisymmetric since, for example, ${1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=0$ and $(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}=0$ but $1\\ne -1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
    {
